---
// Lesson 1.6a — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack
// The Animator as a state machine students can see; parameters the code sets.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.6a — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your player already walks. It does not <em>look</em> alive. Today:
    the Animator window, four parameters, and the moment you realize
    animation is the first state machine you ever build — you just get to
    see this one.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Until now, our player has been animating a walk cycle constantly from <code>Start()</code>,
    even when standing completely still. Today, you write <code>UpdateAnimation()</code> in
    <code>PlayerController.cs</code> to make the animation respond directly to player movement:</p>
    <pre><code>bool isMoving = xDirection != 0 || yDirection != 0;
anim.SetBool("IsWalking", isMoving);

if (isMoving)
{
    if (Mathf.Abs(xDirection) &gt;= Mathf.Abs(yDirection))
    {
        anim.SetInteger("WalkDir", 1); // Side
        sprite.flipX = xDirection &gt; 0;
    }
    else
    {
        if (yDirection &gt; 0)
        {
            anim.SetInteger("WalkDir", 0); // Up
            sprite.flipX = true;
        }
        else
        {
            anim.SetInteger("WalkDir", 2); // Down
            sprite.flipX = false;
        }
    }
}

if (Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0))
{
    anim.SetTrigger("Attack");
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict: the code sets <strong>four</strong> things on the
        Animator — <code>IsWalking</code> (bool), <code>WalkDir</code>
        (int: 0 up, 1 side, 2 down), <code>Attack</code> (trigger) — and
        the sprite's <code>flipX</code>. Draw the four cases
        (standing / walking left-right / walking up / walking down) and
        what the animator should show for each. Check yourself against
        the game after the lab.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The code never says "play the walk frames." It only sets
            <em>state</em> — IsWalking true, WalkDir 2 — and something
            else decides what animation to run. Where is that decision
            actually made, and why is splitting it out like this the same
            idea as the StatManager being separate from the player?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — open the Animator.</strong> Select the Player in
        the Overworld scene, then Window → Animation → Animator. The
        player's Animator Controller opens: boxes (states) connected by
        arrows (transitions). Find the PlayerAnimator child in the
        Hierarchy — the animator lives on the child, which is why the
        code says <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;Animator&gt;()</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — read the conditions.</strong> Click the arrow
        from the side-walk state to the idle state and read the
        Inspector: "Exit when IsWalking is false." Click the transitions
        between up/side/down: they fire on WalkDir equals 0 / 1 / 2.
        The parameters tab (left of the window) lists all three. That is
        the whole contract: <strong>code sets parameters, the Animator
        reads them.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the axe variant.</strong> The Player prefab ships
        with animation variants including one holding an <strong>axe</strong>.
        Find the axe-walk state(s) in the animator. Nothing points at them
        yet — you will wire hasAxe into this machine in
        <a href="5.1a.html">5.1a</a>. Today: just find it, and leave a
        comment in your fork's README about which state names exist.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the walk cycle</h3>
        <p>Break each one in the Animator window, run the game, describe what you see, then fix it:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Set the side→idle transition's condition to "IsWalking
                is true." What does walking do now, and why?</li>
            <li>Delete the WalkDir condition from the up/side transition.
                What happens when you walk diagonally up-right — and
                why did the code's <code>&gt;=</code> matter?</li>
            <li>In the code, comment out the <code>sprite.flipX</code>
                line. Which direction can you never face, and what
                exactly did flipX do?</li>
            <li>Set the Attack trigger from the code — click the mouse.
                The trigger fires once per click: what would break if
                the code used SetBool instead of SetTrigger?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Your animation state diagram</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>On paper, draw every state the Player animator can be in,
                with arrows and the parameter conditions on each arrow.
                How many states does the shipped animator have, and can
                every state reach every other one?</li>
            <li>Write your own C# one-liner that makes the player look
                attacked: where would you put a Hurt trigger if the
                boss unit needed one?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Open the Animator window and read transitions + conditions</li>
            <li>Explain SetBool vs SetInteger vs SetTrigger with the player's parameters as examples</li>
            <li>Explain the code↔Animator split: code sets state, Animator plays frames</li>
            <li>Draw the player's animation state diagram from memory</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — animation states section</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — the same idea, running the enemy (preview it now if you are curious)</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the Animator API rows</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.1a.html">2.1a — Coins: Triggers, Tags &amp; the StatManager</a> — Unit 2 begins.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.6a" title="1.6a Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
